// A hash map, generic in its key and its value.
//
// The axis this owns is **the trait system under load**. A container is where bounds, the
// behaviour they promise, and ownership all have to agree at once: the map may hash and compare a
// key it knows nothing else about, it holds keys and values it did not make, and it hands entries
// back out while still owning them. Nothing else in the set asks for all three at the same time.
//
// It is a chained map — a bucket is a list of entries, and an entry is a `&Entry` so the list can
// hold itself. The bucket array grows by doubling and everything is rehashed, which is the part of
// the problem the language had the most to say about.
//
// What writing it turned up, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **There was no `Hash` in the core catalog.** The prelude had `Eq` and `Ord` — enough to sort
//     with, not enough to key on — so a map had to declare its own, as this one did, along with an
//     `impl` for every key type it wanted to support. That was never a style problem: `02`'s
//     coherence rule lets an `impl` live only with its trait or with its type, so two libraries
//     that each declare a `Hash` can never share a key type's implementation, and a program using
//     both writes the same hash twice. `Hash` is now a catalog trait (`14 §2`), which deleted a
//     trait, two `impl`s, and the avalanche function from this file — the built-in mixing is the
//     same splitmix64 finalizer this used to carry, in the one place every container can reach it.
//   * **An enum has no zero value, so an array of them cannot be declared without one.**
//     `var cells: [16]Option[&Entry[K, V]]` is refused. This one has since been answered: the
//     repeat form `[None; block_size]` fills the array from a value instead of from a zero, and
//     `empty_block` below is now one line. It was sixteen `None`s written out.
//   * **Generically it is worse than that, and still is.** A `[16]K` cannot be declared *whatever*
//     `K` is: a repeat needs a value in its value position, and a type parameter promises none —
//     no bound in the catalog promises a value. A generic container therefore cannot make its own
//     storage unless it already has something to fill it with, which is why the bucket array here
//     holds `Option[…]` rather than entries.
//   * **Storage cannot be asked for at a size chosen while running** (`07`, *Not yet*), so the
//     table is a fixed directory of fixed blocks and its capacity has a ceiling written into the
//     type. The growth is real — blocks are allocated, the mask changes, every entry is rehashed
//     into a new bucket — but it stops at `max_blocks`, after which the map keeps working with
//     longer chains instead.
//   * **A bound is repeated at every declaration that needs it.** `[K: Hash + Eq, V]` is written
//     on the type and again on each free function over it; there is no `where` clause (`10 § Open
//     c`) and no name for a set of bounds.
//   * **An assignment is an expression, and a `match` written for effect is checked as if it were
//     not.** `00 §2` settles that `x = v` yields `v`, and `match`'s arms must agree on one value
//     type — so a match whose arms merely each end in an assignment is refused for disagreeing
//     about a value nobody wanted. An `if` has a rule for exactly this case (one `unit` branch
//     makes the whole thing a statement) and `match` does not; `put` below gets past it with a
//     `return`.
//   * **Two generics cannot solve each other.** `Map(empty_dir(), …)` asks the constructor to fix
//     `Map`'s parameters from its arguments while asking `empty_dir` to fix its own from the field
//     it lands in, and neither can go first — so the binding is annotated. Inference is
//     bidirectional (`10 §4`) but not simultaneous.
//
// What went in without friction is worth recording too, since the point of the exercise is where
// the language stops rather than a list of complaints: the definition-time bound check does what
// `10 §5` promises, so `k.hash()` is refused at the line that writes it when `K: Hash` is missing
// rather than at some instantiation; a user type joins the bound with an ordinary `impl Hash`,
// exactly as it joins `Eq`; and `Cursor` below takes no bounds at all, because walking a table asks
// nothing of a key — which is what having the bound on the declaration that needs it means.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- storage

// One bucket's worth of the table: the entries whose keys landed on the same index, held through
// each other so the list is as long as it needs to be.
struct Entry[K, V]
    key: K
    value: V
    next: Option[&Entry[K, V]]
end Entry

struct Block[K, V]
    cell: [block_size]Option[&Entry[K, V]]
end Block

struct Dir[K, V]
    block: [max_blocks]Option[&Block[K, V]]
end Dir

// How many buckets a block holds, and how many blocks the directory can point at. Between them they
// fix the capacity ceiling at 256 buckets, and because they are constants rather than the nullary
// functions this was first written with, the two arrays above are sized by the same names the code
// below divides by (`13 §7`).
private const block_size: usize = 16

private const max_blocks: usize = 16

// An `Option` has no zero value, so neither array can be declared and left to start at one — but a
// repeat fills them from a value that costs nothing to name, and the count is the same constant the
// rest of the file divides by.
private empty_block[K, V]() -> &Block[K, V] = Block([None; block_size])

private empty_dir[K, V]() -> &Dir[K, V] = Dir([None; max_blocks])

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the map

// A map starts with one block and doubles from there. `blocks` is always a power of two, so the
// bucket count is one as well and an index is a mask rather than a division.
struct Map[K: Hash + Eq, V]
    dir: &Dir[K, V]
    blocks: usize
    count: usize

    buckets(self) -> usize = self.blocks * block_size

    // The block a bucket lives in. Every block below `blocks` is allocated the moment the table
    // grows to it, so this is the one place the invariant is spelled out.
    at(self, i: usize) -> &Block[K, V] = self.dir.block[i / block_size].expect("a live block")

    bucket(self, i: usize) -> Option[&Entry[K, V]] = self.at(i).cell[i % block_size]

    set_bucket(*self, i: usize, e: Option[&Entry[K, V]])
        self.at(i).cell[i % block_size] = e

    index(self, k: K) -> usize = usize(k.hash()) & (self.buckets() - 1)

    // The entry a key names, or `None`. Handing the entry itself back is what lets `get`, `put`,
    // and `has` be three lines each — and it is safe to hand out because the map still holds it.
    find(self, k: K) -> Option[&Entry[K, V]]
        var at = self.bucket(self.index(k))

        loop
            at match
                None -> break None
                Some(e) ->
                    if e.key == k then break Some(e)
                    at = e.next
    end find

    get(self, k: K) -> Option[V]
        self.find(k) match
            None -> None
            Some(e) -> Some(e.value)
    end get

    has(self, k: K) -> bool = self.find(k).is_some()

    // A key already in the map keeps its entry and takes the new value; a fresh one goes at the
    // head of its bucket, which is what makes an insert a constant number of writes.
    //
    // The `return` is not only for reading. An assignment is an expression that yields what it
    // assigned (`00 §2`), so without it the two arms would end at a `V` and a `usize` and have to
    // agree on a type that nothing here wants.
    put(*self, k: K, v: V)
        self.find(k) match
            Some(e) ->
                e.value = v
                return
            None ->
                if self.count * 4 >= self.buckets() * 3 then self.grow()

                var i = self.index(k)
                var e: &Entry[K, V] = Entry(k, v, self.bucket(i))

                self.set_bucket(i, Some(e))
                self.count += 1usize
    end put

    // Unlinking is the one walk that needs to remember where it came from, since the entry before
    // the one being removed is what has to be made to point past it.
    remove(*self, k: K) -> bool
        var i = self.index(k)
        var at = self.bucket(i)
        var prev: Option[&Entry[K, V]] = None

        loop
            at match
                None -> break false
                Some(e) ->
                    if e.key == k
                        prev match
                            None -> self.set_bucket(i, e.next)
                            Some(p) -> p.next = e.next
                        self.count -= 1usize
                        break true
                    prev = Some(e)
                    at = e.next
    end remove

    // Doubling the table changes the mask, so where a key belongs changes with it and every entry
    // has to be looked at. They are **relinked** rather than rebuilt: an entry is already a
    // reference, so moving one between buckets is two writes and no allocation, and the keys and
    // values it holds are never copied.
    //
    // Growth stops at the ceiling rather than failing. A map that has run out of directory keeps
    // answering every question correctly; the chains simply get longer.
    grow(*self)
        if self.blocks >= max_blocks then return

        var old = self.dir
        var old_buckets = self.buckets()

        self.dir = empty_dir()
        self.blocks = self.blocks * 2
        for b in 0usize..<self.blocks
            self.dir.block[b] = Some(empty_block())

        for i in 0usize..<old_buckets
            var at = old.block[i / block_size].expect("a live block").cell[i % block_size]

            loop
                at match
                    None -> break
                    Some(e) ->
                        // The rest of the old chain is read before the entry is relinked, since
                        // relinking is what overwrites the link to it.
                        var rest = e.next
                        var j = self.index(e.key)

                        e.next = self.bucket(j)
                        self.set_bucket(j, Some(e))
                        at = rest
    end grow

    walk(self) -> Cursor[K, V] = Cursor(self.dir, self.buckets(), 0usize, None)
end Map

// An empty map, with its first block already under it so that every later question can assume one.
//
// A nullary generic solves its parameters from what the binding asks for (`10 §4`), which is why
// the key and value types of a map are written on the `var` that holds it. The annotation on `m`
// is the same rule biting one level in: `Map(empty_dir(), …)` would have to solve `Map`'s
// parameters from its arguments and `empty_dir`'s from the field it lands in, and neither can go
// first.
map[K: Hash + Eq, V]() -> Map[K, V]
    var m: Map[K, V] = Map(empty_dir(), 1usize, 0usize)

    m.dir.block[0] = Some(empty_block())
    m
end map

// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ walking

// A position in a table: which bucket to look at next, and where in the current chain.
//
// It takes **no bounds**, and that is the point of writing them where they are needed: walking a
// table hashes nothing and compares nothing, so a cursor asks nothing of a key. It holds the
// directory rather than the map so that it keeps the storage alive while it walks — but the table
// it is walking must not grow underneath it, and a cursor is finished with before the next `put`.
struct Cursor[K, V]
    dir: &Dir[K, V]
    buckets: usize
    at: usize
    cell: Option[&Entry[K, V]]

    next(*self) -> Option[&Entry[K, V]]
        loop
            self.cell match
                Some(e) ->
                    self.cell = e.next
                    break Some(e)
                None ->
                    if self.at >= self.buckets then break None

                    var b = self.dir.block[self.at / block_size].expect("a live block")

                    self.cell = b.cell[self.at % block_size]
                    self.at += 1usize
    end next
end Cursor
